  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CONSERVING BORNEAN ORANGUTANS IN PRIMARY, DISTURBED AND CUSTOMARY FORESTS IN GUNUNG PALUNG NATIONAL PARK, INDONESIA, AND THE SURROUNDING LANDSCAPE</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The purpose of this project is to (1) apply the use of drone technology, including newly available thermal drones, to assess orangutan populations, locate individual animals and assess canopy temperature, and build a photographic database of orangutan nests from a drones perspective; pilot the use of thermal.equipped drones as a method for locating orangutans; implement the use of thermal-equipped drones to rapidly assess orangutan presence in protected Customary Forest areas; and assess canopy temperature using thermal-equipped drones as a measure of degradation and fire risk; (2) Determine and monitor levels of biodiversity and anthropogenic disturbance in primary, degraded and customary forest habitats; investigate differences in biodiversity and orangutan behavior between forest habitats through camera trapping; expand bio-acoustic monitoring in degraded and primary habitats; investigate differences in phenology of trees and lianas in primary, disturbed and Customary Forests; determine differences in rainfall, temperature, humidity and solar radiation across primary and disturbed forests as a measure of anthropogenic disturbance and an indicator of forest fire risk; (3) Understand the impact of human disturbance, activity and proximity on orangutan behavior, diet, and health in different forest types present in the Gunung Palung National Park (GPNP) landscape; determine individual identity of orangutans utilizing primary, degraded and customary forest habitats; establish a semi-permanent camp at the Rangkong Research Site; determine differences in orangutan range use and behavior in degraded vs. primary forest types; determine differences in orangutan diet in primary, degraded and customary forests; measure health and parasite load of orangutans found in the Rangkong and Cabang Panti sites; (4) assist the Tanjung Gunung community decrease their dependence on environmentally destructive activities, switching to more sustainable livelihoods and becoming environ</narrative>
  </description>
